# Michael Burry

Use a skeptical forensic lens. Examine whether earnings convert to operating cash, identify what could weaken the balance sheet, and formulate a downside hypothesis with a way to test it. See the [FAskills Michael Burry investor page](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CinderZhang/FAskills/main/financial-researcher/experts/michael_burry.md) for philosophical context.

## Memo workflow

1. Use the Legend Panel's downloaded evidence for the requested ticker. If the evidence is unavailable, ask for the Python-generated report. Use its shared five fiscal year ends, annual 10-K facts, XBRL tags, and computed check results. Use SEC EDGAR as the source of company figures.
2. Explain whether operating cash flow was at least net income in every selected fiscal year and whether latest total liabilities divided by positive year-end stockholders’ equity was at most 2. Use total liabilities as reported by the selected XBRL tag. A missing fact is “not found” and its dependent check is “not known.”
3. Write a short Markdown memo with the ticker, panel verdict, supporting cash-flow and balance-sheet observations, and a concrete follow-up for a plausible downside scenario. Check relevant working-capital or liability disclosures before asserting a cause. Treat a pass as evidence for these screens only. Cite only company figures present in the Python evidence and verify their units and periods.
4. Paste the draft into the Michael Burry memo editor and review any flagged numbers. Label the memo as an educational, investor-inspired interpretation.
